Similarly for destination.

Goal also includes origin_node_occupied and destination_node_occupied. To get nodes occupied we need to load payload onto origin or destination? Actually loading origin payload sets origin_node_occupied and origin_availability_locked and origin_payload_linked. Unload origin payload clears origin_node_occupied and sets origin_node_ready. Goal wants origin_node_occupied and origin_node_ready both true? Wait goal includes both origin_node_occupied and origin_node_ready. That seems contradictory because loading origin payload sets origin_node_occupied and not origin_node_ready; unload sets origin_node_ready and not origin_node_occupied. However goal includes both occupied and ready. Let's check: goal includes origin_node_occupied origin_node_alpha AND origin_node_ready origin_node_alpha. According to predicates, they are mutually exclusive? Not explicitly but likely they are mutually exclusive via effects: load_origin_payload adds origin_node_occupied and removes? It does not remove origin_node_ready. Actually load_origin_payload effect: (origin_node_occupied ?origin_node) (origin_availability_locked ?origin_availability) (origin_payload_linked ?origin_availability ?payload_unit) (not (cargo_available ?payload_unit)). It does not affect origin_node_ready. So origin_node_ready may remain true if previously set. Similarly unload_origin_payload adds origin_node_ready and removes origin_node_occupied? Actually unload effect: (origin_node_ready ?origin_node) (origin_availability_confirmed ?origin_availability) (cargo_available ?payload_unit) (not (origin_payload_linked ?origin_availability ?payload_unit)). It does not remove origin_node_occupied. So both could be true simultaneously if we load then unload? Let's see: load adds occupied, does not affect ready. If ready was true before load, after load we have occupied & ready. Then unload adds ready (already true) and removes payload link, but does not remove occupied. So after load then unload we have occupied & ready (since